From fb3433bdc3fe84e086341dcc3e23db9724fa4835 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Viet Hung Nguyen <hvn@calif.io>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 00:04:37 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix: Six fixes found by differential fuzzing against Avro C++

The first four were found by comparing this crate against Avro C++ on
generated schemas, the last two by reviewing those. In every case Avro C++
follows the specification, or is simply right, and this crate did not.

1. A union may hold at most one branch per type, except for the named
   types record, fixed and enum. A logical type annotates an underlying
   type, so `time-micros` and `timestamp-micros` are two `long` branches
   and a union of them is no more legal than a union of two plain `long`s.
   Schema gives each logical type its own variant, so the SchemaKind
   discriminant that drove duplicate detection saw them as distinct.

   The underlying type of a decimal comes from its inner schema, so a
   decimal on `bytes` collides with plain `bytes` while one on a `fixed`
   is named and collides with nothing. That also fixes the opposite error:
   a union of a decimal on `fixed` beside a decimal on `bytes` was
   rejected as a duplicate, and Avro C++ accepts it.

   Two branches naming one type are also two branches of the same type, so
   a union may hold neither a definition and a reference to it nor two
   references to one name. variant_index's own note has recorded that gap
   for as long as it has existed.

   Duplicate detection is kept apart from variant_index, which answers a
   different question -- which branch a value of some kind belongs to --
   and has to stay keyed by the exact kind, its documented slow path
   handling logical and named types.

2. A fixed of size zero is rejected. The specification calls `size` "a
   positive integer", a fixed of no bytes can hold no value, and the error
   variant raised here has been named GetFixedSizeFieldPositive all along
   while the check was only that the field parsed as an unsigned integer.

3. A duration renders as one fixed carrying `logicalType`, not as a `type`
   whose value is a fixed. It nested them, producing
   {"type":{"type":"fixed",..},"logicalType":"duration"}, which no
   implementation reads: Avro C++ rejects it with 'Json field "type" is
   not a string' and re-parsing it with this crate failed too, so a
   duration did not survive a round trip through its own rendering. The
   specification does not say what the underlying fixed should be called
   and Schema::Duration carries no name to remember, so a parsed name is
   still lost; that is a separate limit of the model.

4. The document scan skips a vertical tab and a form feed rather than
   reading one as the start of a scalar. Neither is a value under either
   reading -- strict JSON refuses them, Avro C++ skips them as spacing --
   but scan_json fell through to its scalar arm, so a leading vertical tab
   made json_document_end report a boundary of 1 and a caller cutting
   there was left with no document at all. Found after 38 minutes and 24.9
   million runs, by an input that needed a leading vertical tab and a
   trailing NUL together: each alone parsed.

Not additive: one upstream test,
avro_custom_attributes_schema_without_attributes, declared a fixed of size
0. That test is about custom attributes and the size is incidental to it,
so it now declares 16.

Two further fixes, found by reviewing the four above rather than by fuzzing,
since neither is reachable from the generators:

5. Inside a string literal, only a *bare* byte below 0x20 is escaped. One
   that follows a backslash is left alone, because there it is not a
   character an escape denotes: tryString accepts a backslash only before
   one of " \ / b f n r t u and throws on anything else, so Avro C++
   rejects such a document and so does serde_json. Escaping it anyway
   emitted the backslash and then an escape, so a backslash followed by
   0x01 became two backslashes followed by u0001, which parses as one
   escaped backslash plus five ordinary characters -- turning a document
   both readers reject into one this crate accepts, holding text nobody
   wrote.

6. A union branch's name is read from a decimal's inner schema, as its
   underlying type already was. A decimal on a named fixed was checked by
   neither rule: the type rule skipped it as named, and the name rule
   supplied no name because Schema::Decimal has none. Two decimals over
   one fixed, or a decimal beside the bare fixed it wraps, were accepted
   and would then resolve to whichever branch came first.
